lay=("Think of an enhancer as a volume knob and a promoter as an amplifier. If every amplifier turns the same knob setting into more or less loudness, the loudest knob on one amplifier is also the loudest on the others, even if one amplifier clips at high volume. "
 "If different amplifiers listen for different things, the order of the knobs can truly flip. We proved on a computer that clipping alone can make two knobs tie but never swap places. "
 "In published mouse-cell data the order shifts by more than measurement noise allows, though studies disagree on how much. "
 "One extra experiment would tell the stories apart: turn each enhancer up in steps on two contrasting promoters and repeat every measurement three times.")
gl=[("enhancer","a stretch of DNA that turns up a gene when proteins bind it"),("promoter","the DNA switch at the start of a gene where reading begins"),("core promoter","the short bare-minimum part of a promoter, with patterns such as TATA, Inr or DPE"),("TATA, Inr, DPE","short DNA patterns in core promoters that help the reading machinery attach"),("CpG island","a stretch of DNA rich in C-G pairs found at many housekeeping gene starts"),("housekeeping gene","a gene needed in every cell all the time"),("rank order","the list of enhancers sorted from weakest to strongest"),("saturation","an output that levels off because a promoter cannot go any higher"),("multiplicative","two effects combine by multiplying, so a stronger enhancer is stronger by the same factor everywhere"),("latent strength","a single hidden number that would fully describe how strong an enhancer is"),("Spearman correlation","a number from -1 to 1 saying how similar two rankings are; 1 means identical order"),("disattenuated","corrected for measurement noise using repeat measurements, so noise alone is not mistaken for a real change"),("cofactor / coactivator","a helper protein that carries the enhancer's message to the promoter"),("Hill function","a curve that rises and then levels off, used to model saturation"),("topology","how DNA folds in 3D so that distant pieces touch or are kept apart"),("tandem copies","placing the same enhancer 1, 2, 4 or 8 times in a row to turn it up"),("promoter competition","several promoters sharing one enhancer's output so that one gain is another's loss")]
