summary=("Mechanisms that can change enhancer rank order are those adding a second hidden dimension beyond one enhancer strength: core-promoter motif compatibility (TATA, DPE, Inr, CpG; housekeeping versus developmental), cofactor-promoter compatibility, and TF preferences. "
 "Saturation, power-law responsiveness and promoter-independent distance or contact effects are monotone functions of one strength; a compiled core-Lean theorem (Q1.no_reversal) shows they can create ties but never reversals, although with noise they lower the raw rank correlation (simulated raw Spearman 0.76-0.83 with no true change). "
 "The literature disagrees: Martinez-Ara 2022 (mouse) finds over half of CREs promoter-selective; Bergman 2022 (human) finds multiplicative combination (R2 0.82) with subtle class effects; the Tan 2026 preprint finds similar rank order with promoter-specific power-law exponents. "
 "Our re-analysis of Martinez-Ara GEO GSE186265 gives noise-corrected between-promoter Spearman medians of 0.56, 0.83 and 0.62 for three loci (saturation alone predicts 1.0), so a rank-changing component exists there, with the caveats listed. "
 "One added experiment (2 contrasting promoters x 48-192 enhancers x 1-8 tandem copies x 3 replicates, scored by noise-corrected Spearman) separates the one-strength class (predicted about 1.0, flat in dose) from the compatibility class (predicted about 0.6-0.8, flat in dose). It does not separate motif from cofactor from TF compatibility, and does not test topology or promoter competition.")
